# ============================================================================
# ECONOMETRICS QUICK REFERENCE - FORMULAS & KEY CONCEPTS
# ============================================================================
# Glance at this for quick formula lookup during exam
# ============================================================================

# ============================================================================
# 1. FOUNDATIONS
# ============================================================================
# Sample mean:                 x_bar = (1/n) * sum(x_i)
# Change in y:                 Δy = β₁ * Δx
# Multi-variable change:       Δy = β₁*Δx₁ + β₂*Δx₂ + ...
# Percent change:              ((new - old) / old) * 100%
# Percentage point change:     new - old
# NOTE: Intercept β₀ doesn't affect changes; constants drop out when taking Δ

# ============================================================================
# 2. DESCRIBING DATA
# ============================================================================
# Sample variance:             S_x² = (1/(n-1)) * Σ(x_i - x_bar)²
# Sample SD:                   S_x = sqrt(S_x²)
# Sample covariance:           S_xy = (1/(n-1)) * Σ(x_i - x_bar)(y_i - y_bar)
# Correlation:                 ρ = S_xy / (S_x * S_y)
# Range:                       -1 ≤ ρ ≤ 1
# Sign:                        + (together), - (opposite), 0 (none)
# Units:                       Correlation unitless; covariance has units

# ============================================================================
# 3. PROBABILITY & EXPECTATIONS
# ============================================================================
# E[X] (discrete):             E[X] = Σ x_i * P(X = x_i)
# E[X²]:                       E[X²] = Σ x_i² * P(X = x_i)
# E[aX + b]:                   aE[X] + b
# E[aX + bY]:                  aE[X] + bE[Y]
# Var(X):                      E[X²] - (E[X])²
# Var(aX + b):                 a² * Var(X)
# Var(X + Y) if independent:   Var(X) + Var(Y)
# Var(aX + bY) general:        a²Var(X) + b²Var(Y) + 2ab*Cov(X,Y)
# Cov(X,Y):                    E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]
# Cov if independent:          0
# Cov(aX + b, cY + d):         ac * Cov(X,Y)  [constants don't matter]
# Cor(X,Y):                    Cov(X,Y) / (sd(X) * sd(Y))
# E[Y | X = x]:                average of Y when X = x
# Conditional variance:        spread of Y within group where X = x

# ============================================================================
# 4. SIMPLE LINEAR REGRESSION
# ============================================================================
# Model:                       Y = β₀ + β₁X + U
# Fitted value:                ŷ = β̂₀ + β̂₁x
# Residual:                    û = y - ŷ
# OLS slope:                   β̂₁ = cov(x,y) / var(x)
# OLS intercept:               β̂₀ = ȳ - β̂₁ * x̄
# Predicted change:            Δŷ = β̂₁ * Δx
# Zero Conditional Mean (ZCM): E[U | X] = 0  ⟹  E[Y | X] = β₀ + β₁X

# ============================================================================
# 5. GOODNESS OF FIT
# ============================================================================
# SST (total sum of squares):  Σ(y_i - ȳ)²
# SSR (sum squared residuals): Σ(y_i - ŷ_i)²
# SSE (explained sum of squares): Σ(ŷ_i - ȳ)²
# Decomposition:               SST = SSE + SSR
# R²:                          SSE / SST = 1 - (SSR / SST)
# Range:                       0 ≤ R² ≤ 1
# Interpretation:              Fraction of variation in Y explained by X
# NOTE:                        Low R² is common in economics (not bad)

# ============================================================================
# QUICK R COMMANDS
# ============================================================================
# mean(x)                  # Mean
# var(x)                   # Variance (divides by n-1)
# sd(x)                    # SD
# cov(x, y)                # Covariance
# cor(x, y)                # Correlation
# sum(values * probs)      # Expected value

# ============================================================================
# KEY CONCEPTUAL POINTS - MEMORIZE THESE
# ============================================================================
# • Correlation ≠ Causation → confounding variables drive both
# • Ceteris paribus = holding other factors fixed
# • ZCM fails if X is correlated with omitted factors in U
# • β₀ = y-intercept (value of y when x=0); doesn't affect slopes or changes
# • β₁ = ceteris paribus effect of X on Y (the slope)
# • Residuals always sum to zero: Σû_i = 0
# • Point (x̄, ȳ) always lies on OLS line
# • x and residuals always uncorrelated (by OLS construction)
# • R² measures explanatory power (not causal strength)

# ============================================================================
# INTERPRETATION QUICK GUIDE
# ============================================================================
# Positive residual (û > 0):   Model under-predicted (actual > fitted)
# Negative residual (û < 0):   Model over-predicted (actual < fitted)
# High R²:                     Model explains most variation in Y
# Low R²:                      Model explains little, but may still be useful
# Positive correlation:        Variables move in same direction
# Negative correlation:        Variables move in opposite directions
# Zero correlation:            No linear relationship

# ============================================================================
# COMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID
# ============================================================================
# ✗ Confusing β₀ effect: β₀ affects level of ŷ, not slope or Δy
# ✗ Using wrong denominator: Sample var/SD divide by (n-1), not n
# ✗ Forgetting covariance term in Var(aX+bY)
# ✗ Confusing Cov with Cor: Cov has units, Cor doesn't
# ✗ Interpreting low R² as bad: Economics data is noisy; low R² common
# ✗ Assuming correlation means causation: Need ZCM assumption
# ✗ Forgetting ZCM assumption: E[U|X]=0 is crucial for causal interpretation
